# Line endings: store every text file in git with LF ("\n") endings.
#
# WHY: Windows editors save with CRLF ("\r\n"). Without this file, opening and
# saving ci.yml on Windows made git report every single line as changed
# (that is what happened in October 2026). With it, git converts on commit,
# so diffs show only the lines you really changed.
#
# SYNTAX: <pattern> <attribute>=<value>
#   text=auto  -> git decides which files are text
#   eol=lf     -> text files are stored with LF in the repository
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts genuinely need CRLF.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary files: never touch their bytes.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary
*.pbix binary
*.duckdb binary
